                              WHATSNEW.PPL
              Items added to PPL for version 3.20

============================================================================

  STATEMENT KILLMSG INTEGER confnum, INTEGER msgnum

  Parameters : confnum = conference number in which the doomed message resides.
               msgnum  = message number to kill

  EXAMPLE    :

              KILLMSG 10,10234


  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION INTEGER FTELL(INTEGER channel)

  Parameters : channel - The file channel to process

  Return Val : 4 byte signed integer containing the file pointer offet
               of the file attached to channel.

  EXAMPLE    :
               FOPEN 1,"C:\MYFILE.TXT",O_RD,S_DN
               FSEEK 1,10,SEEK_SET
               PRINTLN "Current file offset for MYFILE.TXT is ",FTELL(1)
               FCLOSE 1

  Description: FTELL returns the current file pointer offset for the specified
               file channel. If the channel is not open, it will return 0.
               Otherwise it will return the cirrent postion in the open file.

  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION INTEGER OS()

  Parameters : None

  Return Val : An Integer indicating which operating system/ pcboard version
               the PPE is currently running under. 1=DOS, 2 = OS2, 0 = unknown.

  Example    :

               SELECT CASE ( OS() )
                   CASE 1: PRINTLN "Running DOS version of PCBoard"
                   CASE 2: PRINTLN "Running OS/2 version of Pcboard"
               END SELECT


  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION BS2I  Converts a 4 byte bsreal to a PPL integer.
        Syntax  INTEGER  BS2I(BIGSTR bsreal)
        parameters:  bsreal is a BIGSTR type since BIGSTR types can contain
                     binary data. For this function, PPL will convert the first
                     4 bytes of the BIGSTR into an INTEGER variable and retun
                     it.

      Return value:  Returns a converted 4 byte bsreal in the form of a 4 byte
                     integer

      Example:

        BIGSTR str1
        INTEGER int1

        fopen 1,"d:\test\pcb\main\net", O_RD,S_DN
        fread 1,str1,4

        int1 = BS2I(str1)
        println "integer after conversion = ", int1

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION BD2I  Converts an 8 byte bdreal to a PPL integer
        See BS2I
        Example:

        BIGSTR str1
        INTEGER int1

        fopen 1,"d:\test\pcb\main\net", O_RD,S_DN
        fread 1,str1,8

        int1 = BS2I(str1)
        println "integer after conversion = ", int1

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION I2BS  Converts a 4 byte PPL INTEGER into a 4 byte bsreal and stores it
        in a BIGSTR.

        Syntax:  BIGSTR I2BS(INTEGER value)
        Params:  value- a PPL INTEGER value
        returns: A BIGSTR containing the binary immage of the bsreal

        EXAMPLE:

        BIGSTR str1
        INTEGER int1

        int1 = 37;
        str1 = I2BS(int1)

        fopen 1,"d:\test\pcb\main\net", O_RD,S_DN
        fwrite 1,str1,4


  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FUNCTION I2BD  Converts a 4 byte PPL INTEGER into an 8 byte bdreal and stores it
        in a BIGSTR
        See I2BS

  --------------------------------------------------------------------------

STATEMENT  SOUNDDELAY INTEGER frequency, INTEGER duration
      Params: frequency = frequency at which to sound the PC speaker
              duration  = length, in clock ticks (18 = 1 second), to leave
                          the speaker on

      EXAMPLE:

      If (inputval != validInputVal) SOUNDDELAY 500,18

      Comments: This function was added to replace the
                SOUND 500
                SOUND 0
                combination required for DOS, since this functionality
                is not available under OS/2.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATEMENT  USELMRS BOOLEAN uselmrs
      Params: uselmrs = Instructs PCBoard NOT to load an alternate users LMRS
                        when a GETALTUSER is executed.

      EXAMPLE:
      USELMRS FAlSE

      GETLALTUSER 300

      println " I still have plenty of memory left for this PPE because I "
      println " didn't have to load user # 300's Last Message Read Pointers"

      USELMRS TRUE
      GETALTUSER 300

      println "I'll problably never see this message bacause I didn't have"
      println "enough memory to lead user #300's Last Message read Pointers."

    Comments:
      This statment can save a significant amount of memory when a GETALTUSER
      is executed at a later time. When GETALTUSER is executed, it loads
      the users LMR's by default. If you have a significant number of conferences
      on your system, this can require a great deal of memory. Since PCBoard
      is so rich with features, it can take most if not all available
      conventional memory, leaving PPEs out to dry. If an alternate users
      LMRs are not needed by the PPE application, then you can use this
      statement to tell PCBoard not to load the LMR data.

      See also the FUNCTION USELMRS, this will return the current status
      of USELMRS. Eg, if the funtion USELMRS returns TRUE, then a GETALTUSEr
      will load LMRS, if it returns FALSE, LMRS will not be loaded.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATEMENT ADDUSER STRING username,BOOLEAN uservars
      Params: username = name of the new user to add
              uservars = TRUE instructs PCBoard to leave the new user's
                         variables active, as if a GETALTUSER were executed
                         using the new user record number.
                         FALSE will restore the current users variables.


    Comments: This statement allows PPL to create a new user record, filling
              in all fields but the name with pcboard default values.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FUNCTION STRING TINKEY(INTEGER ticks)

      Params: ticks: Number of clock ticks to wait for input.
  Return Val: input entered by user


    Comments: Waits for input from a user.


     Example:

      STRING  str

      PRINTLN "Please enter your response within 10 seconds"
      str = TINKEY(182)

 Comments:
     ticks is the number of clock ticks you wish TINEKY to wait for input
     before timing out. 1 second = 18 ticks (approx)

     A tick value of 0 will cause TINKEY to wait indefinatly for input with
     a maximum timout time of about 4 hours. Carrier loss will also terminate
     TINKEY.







