FINDFIRST()   Function   (3.10)
==============================================================================
  Function

    This function determines if there are any files matching a specified pattern.

  Syntax

    FINDFIRST(file)
      file    A string expression with the path and file name to access information about.  Quite often this expression involves a DOS wildcard (e.g., *.*, *.BAT, etc.)

    Return Type & Value
      STRING  Returns the first filename matching the filename criteria.

    Remarks

      This function is designed to help locate files matching a specific
      criteria.  For example, you may want to delete all files matching
      *.BAK in the current directory.  This can be done easily because
      FINDFIRST() locates the first match, while FINDNEXT() locates
      additional matches.

      It should be noted that only the filenames are returned.  If you need
      additional information such as date, time, or size of the file, use
      the FILEINF() function.

    Example

      STRING file2Delete
      file2Delete = FINDFIRST("*.BAK")
      WHILE (file2Delete != "") DO
        DELETE file2Delete
        PRINTLN file2Delete, " has been deleted."
        file2Delete = FINDNEXT()
      ENDWHILE

    See Also

      FINDNEXT() Function, EXIST() Function, FILEINF() Function

FINDNEXT() Function   (3.10)
==============================================================================
  Function

    This function determines if there are any more files matching a
    specified pattern.

  Syntax

    FINDNEXT()
      No arguments are required.

  Return Type & Value

    STRING  Returns the next filename matching the filename criteria or an
            empty string if there are no more matching files.

  Remarks

    This function is designed to continue where the FINDFIRST() function
    leaves off because it locates any additional files matching the pattern
    last searched for.  There are no more matching files when the return
    value is null or an empty string. Because you do not know how many
    matching files there are, a WHILE loop is usually involved in gathering
    all of the filenames.

    It should be noted that only the filenames are returned.  If you need
    additional information such as date, time, or size of the file, use the
    FILEINF() function.

  Example

    STRING file2Delete
    file2Delete = FINDFIRST("*.BAK")
    WHILE (file2Delete != "") DO
      DELETE file2Delete
      PRINTLN file2Delete, " has been deleted."
      file2Delete = FINDNEXT()
    ENDWHILE

  See Also

    FINDFIRST Function, EXIST() Function, FILEINF() Function


===============================================================================
Bug Fixes:

    SCANMSGHDR: Now uses the correct message number when searching messages
                Also closes all message base files when done.


    DBASE MEMO: DBASE MEMO fields are now working correctly

    Nested SELCT CASE statements have been fixed.

    There are several STATEMENTS/FUNCTIONS in PPL that are not supported
    in the OS/2 version. One of these is the SOUND statement. Under OS/2,
    the SOUND statement will beep at the frequency specified for 1/4 second.
    Sorry about that, it's the price paid of porting existing code to another
    platform. To get consistent functionality, look at the SOUNDDELAY statement.
    This will work the same under DOS and OS/2.

    Here is a list of other statements. functions that are not supported and
    will be ignored by the OS/2 version.


